./bootstrap.sh

last stable build: 2026-08-13


  bootstrap (fresh machine)
-----------------------------

1. install nix

```bash
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf -L https://install.determinate.systems/nix | sh -s -- install
```

2. install homebrew

```bash
/bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)"
```

3. clone dotfiles

```bash
git clone https://github.com/spikedoanz/dotfiles ~/.config/dotfiles
cd ~/.config/dotfiles
```

4. repair (if needed), build, and deploy

```bash
./bootstrap.sh
```

this single command:
- repairs a reboot-broken Nix APFS volume and daemon
- builds the flake as the current user (avoids root Git ownership failures)
- uses sudo only to switch and activate the built generation
- installs all nix packages
- installs all homebrew packages
- symlinks all config files
- sets up tmux plugins
- configures the system



  notes  
---------

macOS Mission Control binds Ctrl+Alt+Arrow to "Move left/right a space" by default.
This conflicts with VsCoq (Coq IDE) keybindings. Disable with:

```bash
defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add 80 '{ enabled = 0; value = { parameters = (65535, 123, 8781824); type = standard; }; }'
defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add 82 '{ enabled = 0; value = { parameters = (65535, 124, 8781824); type = standard; }; }'
```

requires logout/login to take effect.

nix boot repair:

macOS updates/reboots can leave the Nix APFS volume unmounted because the
`org.nixos.*` launch daemons are no longer loaded. The shell runs
`nix-repair-boot --auto` once per boot when `/nix` or the daemon socket is
missing. Run it manually if needed:

```bash
nix-repair-boot
```

you normally do not need to call this directly: `./bootstrap.sh` runs it before
building whenever nix is unavailable.

if it still fails, enable the `sh` items in system settings -> general -> login
items & extensions -> allow in the background.
